WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:04.270
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:10.480
A couple episodes ago, we finally set sail
in the wonderful world of hair simulations.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.110
And today we shall continue our journey in
this domain.

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:17.850
But this time, we are going to talk about
hair modeling.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:22.800
So first, what is the difference between hair
simulation and modeling?

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:29.230
Well, simulation is about trying to compute
the physical forces that act on hair strands,

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:32.900
thereby showing to the user, how they would
move about in reality.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:38.520
Modeling, however, is about obtaining geometry
information from a photograph.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:42.700
This geometry information we can use in our
movies and computer games.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:47.690
We can also run simulations on them and see
how they look on a digital character.

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:53.940
Just think about it, the input is one photograph
and the output is a digital 3D model.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:57.120
This sounds like a remarkably difficult problem.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:02.450
Typically something that a human would do
quite well at, but it would be too labor-intensive

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:05.250
to do so for a large number of hairstyles.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:10.600
Therefore, as usual, neural networks enter
the fray by looking at the photograph, and

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:15.170
trying to estimate the densities and distributions
of the hair strands.

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:21.230
The predicted results are then matched with
the hairstyles found in public data repositories,

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:24.000
and the closest match is presented to the
user.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.240
You can see some possible distribution classes
here.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:32.930
Not only that, but the method is fully automatic,
which means that unlike most previous works,

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:37.070
it doesn't need any guidance from the user
to accomplish this task.

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:43.670
As a result, the authors created an enormous
dataset with 50 thousand photographs and their

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:47.630
reconstructions that they made freely available
for everyone to use.

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:54.250
The output results are so spectacular, it's
almost as if we were seeing magic unfold before

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:55.430
our eyes.

00:01:55.430 --> 00:02:00.330
The fact that we have so many hairstyles in
this dataset also opens up the possibility

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:05.350
of editing, which is always quite a treat
for artists working in the industry.

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:10.670
The main limitation is a poorer reconstruction
of regions that are not visible in the input

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:12.650
photograph, but I think that goes without
saying.

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:18.470
It is slightly ameliorated by the fact that
the public repositories contain hairstyles

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:23.580
that make sense, so we can expect results
of reasonable quality even for the regions

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:26.300
we haven't seen in the input photograph.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:30.690
As always, please let me know below in the
comments section whether you have found everything

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:32.800
understandable in this episode.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.410
Was it easy to digest?

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:36.620
Was there something that was not easy to follow?

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:39.900
Your feedback, as always, is greatly appreciated.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:43.860
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

